-- title:
--   First r-adic coordinate divides r when rmidnrd
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$ and rationals $a,b$, and let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ which is an order in the sense of the project's predicate `IsOrder`: $1\in\Lambda$, $\Lambda$ is closed under multiplication, the $\mathbb{Q}$-span of $\Lambda$ is the whole algebra, and $\Lambda$ is finitely generated over $\mathbb{Z}$. Assume in addition that the image of every integer lies in $\Lambda$. Let $\mathrm{coord}:\Lambda\to W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})\times W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})$ be a coordinate map for $\Lambda$ at $r$, i.e. satisfy `IsOrderCoord`: it is additive; it sends $1$ to $(1,0)$; it is multiplicative in the twisted sense that for $m,m'\in\Lambda$ the coordinates of $mm'$ are $\bigl(\alpha\alpha'+r\,\beta\,\sigma(\beta'),\ \alpha\beta'+\beta\,\sigma(\alpha')\bigr)$, where $(\alpha,\beta)=\mathrm{coord}\,m$, $(\alpha',\beta')=\mathrm{coord}\,m'$ and $\sigma$ is the Witt vector Frobenius; it is injective; its image is dense, in that for every $k$ and every pair $(\alpha,\beta)$ some $m\in\Lambda$ has both coordinates congruent to $\alpha,\beta$ modulo $r^k$; and it satisfies the trace clause $\alpha+\sigma(\alpha)=n$ whenever $m+\bar m=n$ with $n\in\mathbb{Z}$. Then for $m\in\Lambda$ and $n\in\mathbb{Z}$ with $m\,\bar m = rn$ in $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$, the first coordinate $(\mathrm{coord}\,m).1$ lies in the ideal generated by $r$ in $W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})$.
--
--   In the local description of a quaternionic order at a ramified prime $r$, where $\Lambda\otimes\mathbb{Z}_r$ is identified with $W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})\oplus W(\mathbb{F}_{r^2})\Pi$, this is the statement that an element of reduced norm divisible by $r$ lies in the two-sided maximal ideal $\Pi\mathcal{O}_D$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, in the analysis of Atkin–Lehner quotients and rigidifications of fake elliptic curves.
